summary:
Answer: there is no production-grade open-source LLM shopping or comparison assistant worth copying. The best GitHub results are small tutorials, for example ShoppingGPT (162 stars, last push 2024) and REcall (21 stars). MYEZ's own price pipeline is already ahead of the open-source price trackers: its JSON-LD parser handles AggregateOffer, which changedetection.io's extractor does not. What is worth borrowing is patterns and schemas, not code:
(1) Robotoff's prediction, insight and per-device vote model, for the self-growing knowledge base and genuine feedback questions;
(2) Shopify's official UCP Catalog MCP (search_catalog at /api/ucp/mcp), a sanctioned way to search Shopify stores;
(3) Splink plus RapidFuzz (both MIT) for offline product matching, with Claude Haiku judging only the ambiguous pairs, which the published research supports;
(4) Claude citations for pros/cons grounding, keeping in mind that citations cannot be combined with structured outputs;
(5) Inspect AI or promptfoo (MIT) for the evaluation loop;
(6) the Open Prices price fields (discount, proof, duplicate_of);
(7) a GTIN check-digit rule;
(8) react-native-share plus react-native-view-shot (MIT) for per-platform sharing. These are native modules, so they wait for 1.0.1.
Licences: the Open Food Facts server, Robotoff, Open Prices, SearXNG and Unleash are AGPL-3.0, so borrow ideas only. PriceBuddy's licence bans commercial use. PriceGhost has no licence.
Open data cannot seed the fragrance knowledge base: Open Beauty Facts has 447 perfumes and no Gulf fragrance houses in its brand list, and Wikidata has 131 perfume items with a manufacturer. The knowledge base has to be owned.
One in-repo anti-pattern should not be copied into new knowledge-base tables: migration 012 gives the three cache tables anon-writable policies (USING true / WITH CHECK true), already logged as CD-db-rls-03 and still unchanged in the repo. New tables should copy spec_spine's service-role-only policy.
Delta from session 80: none of the session-80 research or r0 JSON files mention any GitHub or open-source repo (grep returned nothing), so everything here is new. GitHub figures are from the GitHub API, read 2026-10-22.
